---
/**
 * The shell every page of this site is served under: the wordmark, where the
 * reader currently is, the site's own navigation, search, and the theme switch.
 *
 * Rebuilt a second time for #542 phase 2, this time on real shadcn
 * components rather than only their variable vocabulary: the mobile
 * collapse is `Sheet` (`SiteNavSheet.tsx`), search is `Command` inside a
 * `Dialog` (`SearchCommand.tsx`, mounted through `DocsSearch.astro`), and the
 * theme switch is a `DropdownMenu` (`ThemeToggleIsland.tsx`, mounted through
 * `ThemeToggle.astro`). This file's own job stays what it was: the wordmark,
 * the breadcrumb, and the site nav, laid out in one row.
 *
 * `bg-*`, `text-*`, `border-*` and similar classes below read shadcn's
 * variable names, which are aliased onto this site's own `--color-*` roles in
 * `src/styles/shadcn-theme.css` — not to values of their own. See that file
 * for the mapping. Spacing, type size and duration are not part of that seam,
 * so they stay literal token references via Tailwind's arbitrary-value syntax
 * (`[var(--space-3)]` and so on) rather than Tailwind's own scale, which is
 * numerically close to this site's in places and would still be a second
 * literal sitting next to the first — exactly what `DESIGN.md` rule 1 rules
 * out.
 *
 * ---- the site navigation ----------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The site's sections, the same list on every page, named in `destinations`
 * below rather than counted here — a number written beside a list is a claim
 * that goes stale the first time the list moves, and this one already has.
 * The workbench is not among them and will not be: the
 * maintainer ruled that it is not to be a public surface, and ruled the same
 * on a Storybook link. This list is the site's own pages.
 *
 * Guides leads, because it is the section a reader arriving with no context
 * reads first — what a part is and how to style one, before which package
 * ships which part. It is also the one whose absence from this strip would
 * matter most: its documents were reachable only by cloning the monorepo and
 * running the workbench until they became routes of this site, and that is the
 * whole reason they are routes at all.
 *
 * `aria-current="page"` marks the section the reader is inside, on a link
 * among sibling links. A reference document therefore carries two current
 * markers — this nav's `Reference`, and the rail's entry for the package
 * being read — in differently labelled landmarks, which is not the ambiguity
 * the breadcrumb note below warns about (two markers for the *same* trail).
 *
 * The section is derived from `Astro.url.pathname` rather than passed in, so a
 * page added later is marked without being told to mark itself, and a page
 * cannot claim a section it is not served from. The `BASE_URL` prefix is
 * stripped first: the site ships from the apex, where the prefix is `/` and a
 * comparison against a literal would look identical, and `astro build --base`
 * is what makes the difference observable (#435).
 *
 * At 40rem (640px) and above the links sit inline beside the wordmark, in
 * normal flow. Below that, a reader with a script gets them drawn inside
 * `SiteNavSheet`'s sheet, reached through the trigger button beside it; a
 * reader without one gets a `<noscript>` list beside it instead, drawn from
 * the same `destinations` array (#591) — no destination depends on the
 * script. CSS visibility, keyed to the same breakpoint across all three
 * renderings, is what keeps exactly one of them visible at a time (see that
 * component's own doc comment, and the one beside the `<nav>` below, for why
 * `e2e/site-nav.spec.ts` still finds exactly one set of links). The
 * platform's own `<details>` this used to be built from bought a focus trap,
 * an Escape handler and the announced open/closed state for free; `Sheet`
 * buys the same three from a Radix dialog instead, which is what the
 * maintainer asked this phase to spend on rather than a second hand-rolled
 * disclosure.
 *
 * Search is on documentation pages only, which is what the `documentation`
 * prop says. It defaults to true — a page added later is documentation unless
 * it says otherwise — and `Base.astro` takes a prop of the same name for the
 * other half of the same decision. The two are separate props for the same
 * reason as before: the layout cannot pass anything to a component a page
 * renders into its slot.
 *
 * The whole header is `data-pagefind-ignore`, for the reason it always was:
 * its words are the same on every page, so indexed they would swamp every
 * result's excerpt with the chrome rather than the document.
 *
 * Deliberately not sticky — the rail is the element a reader actually
 * navigates a long document with, and one sticky element per page is the
 * budget `DESIGN.md` allows.
 *
 * ---- the wordmark and the crumb --------------------------------------------
 *
 * `crumbs` is the path from the site root to this page, not including the root
 * itself, and its last entry is always the page the reader is on. An entry with
 * an `href` is a link; the last one has none, which is what marks it as the
 * page rather than a destination.
 *
 * As of this phase, a crumb no longer repeats a site-nav destination's own
 * label as an ancestor: `src/pages/reference/[pkg].astro` and
 * `src/pages/providers/[provider].astro` used to pass `Reference` and
 * `Provider setup` as a linked ancestor ahead of the page's own name, which
 * put that word twice in the header — once there, bold and adjacent to it,
 * once in the nav below with `aria-current`. The nav already says which
 * section the reader is in, so that ancestor was dead weight rather than
 * information, and both callers now pass only the leaf. A section's own index
 * page (`/reference/`, `/providers/`) still shows its name once, as the
 * trail's leaf — that is the page's own title rather than a repeated
 * ancestor, and removing it would leave the page with no way back to `/` at
 * all (see below).
 *
 * The wordmark is the root of that path, so it is the trail's first item and
 * sits inside the breadcrumb landmark rather than beside it. A trail whose
 * first named ancestor is `Reference` claims the site has no root.
 *
 * `aria-current="page"` is deliberately absent here, for the same reason
 * given above: the rail's current-package marker is the one that does that
 * job on a reference page, and a second one on a non-interactive `span` would
 * put two current-page markers in one document. The leaf is identified
 * structurally instead — last in the trail and not a link.
 *
 * On `/` the list is empty and the trail is not rendered at all, wordmark
 * included: there is nowhere for it to return to, and a strip naming the page
 * directly above the page's own `h1` of the same name would be that word twice
 * in eighty pixels.
 *
 * This used to give a second reason — that the `h1` was already the wordmark
 * "at the title rung" — and it is not one any more. `index.astro` sets that
 * heading at `--text-lg` in the muted ink and spends the display rung on the
 * thesis under it, so the repetition is the whole of the argument now. What is
 * unchanged is the constraint underneath: the heading reads exactly `Playdeck`,
 * `scripts/check-deploy-artifact.mjs` identifies the site's root document by
 * it, and so nothing in this header may be promoted to a heading of that name.
 */
import DocsSearch from './DocsSearch.astro';
import ThemeToggle from './ThemeToggle.astro';
import SiteNavSheet from './SiteNavSheet';
import { cn } from '../lib/utils';

interface Crumb {
  label: string;
  href?: string;
}

interface Props {
  crumbs?: Crumb[];
  documentation?: boolean;
}

const { crumbs = [], documentation = true } = Astro.props;

// Through `import.meta.env.BASE_URL` rather than as a literal, for the reason
// `src/pages/index.astro` gives at length: the prefix is the root today, so a
// hand-written path would be identical and would stop being identical silently
// (#435).
const homeUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const atHome = crumbs.length === 0;

// The section the reader is in: the first segment of this page's path with the
// deployment prefix taken off. `/` and `/design/` are in none of the sections
// listed below, so they simply match nothing.
const path = Astro.url.pathname;
const section = (
  path.startsWith(homeUrl) ? path.slice(homeUrl.length) : path.replace(/^\//, '')
).split('/')[0];

const destinations = [
  { label: 'Guides', segment: 'guides' },
  { label: 'Reference', segment: 'reference' },
  { label: 'Providers', segment: 'providers' },
  { label: 'Examples', segment: 'examples' }
].map(({ label, segment }) => ({
  label,
  href: `${homeUrl}${segment}/`,
  current: segment === section
}));
---

<header
  class="flex items-center gap-[var(--space-3)] [border-block-end:var(--line-width)_solid_var(--color-border)] [padding-block:var(--space-2)] [padding-inline:var(--space-5)]"
  data-pagefind-ignore
>
  {
    /* The shrinkable group. `min-w-0` is what lets it clip instead of forcing
     * the row wider — a flex item's default minimum width is its content
     * size, not zero, so without this the trail would push everything after
     * it off the row rather than truncate. Kept even on `/`, where nothing
     * renders inside it, so the nav and the tools group keep the same
     * position whether or not a trail is present. */
  }
  <div class="flex min-w-0 items-baseline overflow-hidden">
    {
      !atHome && (
        <nav class="min-w-0 [font-size:var(--text-sm)]" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="m-0 flex min-w-0 list-none [gap:var(--space-2)] p-0">
            <li class="flex-none">
              <a
                class="[font-size:var(--text-md)] font-semibold text-foreground no-underline [letter-spacing:var(--tracking-tight)] hover:text-primary active:text-muted-foreground"
                href={homeUrl}
              >
                Playdeck
              </a>
            </li>
            {crumbs.map(({ label, href }) => (
              <li class="crumb min-w-0 flex-none overflow-hidden text-ellipsis whitespace-nowrap text-muted-foreground last:flex-auto last:font-semibold last:text-foreground">
                {href === undefined ? (
                  <span>{label}</span>
                ) : (
                  <a
                    class="text-muted-foreground no-underline hover:text-primary active:text-muted-foreground"
                    href={href}
                  >
                    {label}
                  </a>
                )}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </nav>
      )
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* Beside the wordmark, not spread across the row: `shrink-0` and no grow,
     * so it sits directly after the trail's box with only the header's own
     * `gap` between them.
     *
     * On `/` there is no wordmark and no trail to sit beside (see the
     * doc comment at the top of this file for why) — an empty box, this nav,
     * then a wide gap to the tools at the far end read as an unfinished row
     * rather than a composed one. `atHome` gives this nav the same `ms-auto`
     * the tools group below carries, so the two travel together as one
     * cluster at the row's end instead of standing at opposite corners of it;
     * the wordmark and thesis directly under the hero carry the brand this
     * row would otherwise be asked to.
     *
     * Three renderings of the same list rather than one disclosure: an
     * inline `<ul>`, shown from 40rem up; `SiteNavSheet`'s trigger plus
     * sheet, shown below it once a script has mounted; and a `<noscript>`
     * list drawn from the same `destinations`, for a reader below that width
     * with no script to mount the sheet with (#591 — that reader used to have
     * no header navigation at all, Guides among the losses). The invariant is
     * not "one list, hidden below 40rem" — it is that exactly one set of
     * links is visible inside this landmark at every width, with or without
     * a script, which is what lets `e2e/site-nav.spec.ts` count them without
     * knowing either. `min-[40rem]:hidden` on both the sheet's trigger and
     * the noscript list is what keeps the inline list the only one on screen
     * from 40rem up; below it, the sheet's own content does not exist in the
     * DOM until opened, and the noscript list exists only when no script
     * runs to hide it and mount the sheet in its place — with one, the
     * browser parses everything inside `<noscript>` as inert text rather
     * than markup, so it contributes no `<a>` elements at all. */
  }
  <nav
    class={cn('shrink-0 [font-size:var(--text-sm)]', atHome && 'ms-auto')}
    aria-label="Site"
  >
    <ul class="m-0 hidden list-none [gap:var(--space-4)] p-0 min-[40rem]:flex">
      {destinations.map(({ label, href, current }) => (
        <li>
          <a
            class={cn(
              'inline-block py-[var(--space-1)] text-muted-foreground no-underline hover:text-primary hover:underline active:text-muted-foreground',
              current && 'font-semibold text-primary'
            )}
            href={href}
            aria-current={current ? 'page' : undefined}
          >
            {label}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <SiteNavSheet client:only="react" destinations={destinations} />
    {
      /* The fallback for a reader with no script: `SiteNavSheet` is
       * `client:only="react"`, so without a script it renders nothing to the
       * page at all, and a `<noscript>` written inside that component would
       * never reach the page either — a `client:only` island emits no server
       * HTML to carry it. So this has to live here, in the component that
       * does render server-side, mapping the same `destinations` the two
       * renderings above already use rather than a second hand-written list.
       * `min-[40rem]:hidden` matches the sheet trigger's own class, so above
       * that width this list stays off screen and the inline `<ul>` above is
       * the only one visible. */
    }
    <noscript>
      <ul class="m-0 flex list-none flex-col [gap:var(--space-1)] p-0 min-[40rem]:hidden">
        {destinations.map(({ label, href, current }) => (
          <li>
            <a
              class={cn(
                'inline-block py-[var(--space-1)] text-muted-foreground no-underline hover:text-primary hover:underline active:text-muted-foreground',
                current && 'font-semibold text-primary'
              )}
              href={href}
              aria-current={current ? 'page' : undefined}
            >
              {label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </noscript>
  </nav>

  {
    /* Search and the switch travel together at the far end of the row, and
     * neither shrinks. `ms-auto` pushes the group to the end of the flex
     * container by consuming the remaining free space as margin, which is
     * what keeps the trail and the nav hugging the left edge instead of being
     * spread apart by a growing flex item between them.
     *
     * Only carried here when the nav above is not already carrying it: two
     * flex items each claiming `margin-inline-start: auto` do not let the
     * first absorb all the free space and leave the second with none, they
     * split it, which on `/` reopened the exact gap `atHome` on the nav
     * above was written to close. So this group takes the auto margin only
     * when the nav does not. */
  }
  <div
    class={cn(
      'flex shrink-0 items-center gap-[var(--space-3)]',
      !atHome && 'ms-auto'
    )}
  >
    {documentation && <DocsSearch />}
    <ThemeToggle />
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  /* The generated `/` between crumbs, NOT hidden from assistive technology:
   * generated content is announced, so the trail is heard as the path it
   * draws — root, separator, ancestor, separator, leaf. Between crumbs only;
   * the wordmark, the trail's root, has nothing before it to be separated
   * from. */
  .crumb + .crumb::before {
    margin-inline-end: var(--space-2);
    content: '/';
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }
</style>
